[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is Oral, the discovery brain for self-hosted music. Here\'s the dirty secret of running your own music server. You own everything and you discover nothing. The algorithm was the one thing Spotify did that your Lidarr stack couldn\'t. Oral fixes exactly that.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Personalized recommendations, rotating discovery flows, a release radar, even nearby concerts built on top of Lidarr instead of replacing it. Over a million pulls say the self-hosted crowd agrees.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Even this website is the whole vibe. Look at it. Look. Here\'s the actual app and it looks nothing like the terminal cosplay marketing site. A clean, modern discover page, personalized'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recommendations up top, trends, tags, recent releases, discover playlists, and nearby shows pulled from Ticketmaster. Everything is one click from your library. See an artist you like? Add'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them to Lidarr with your quality default already applied and your existing stack takes it from there. Track previews stream before anything downloads, so curiosity stays free. Disk space only spends when you commit. The builder is'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Lee Kelly from Florida. Nearly 1,500 commits on Oral since January with a semantic release pipeline handling the versioning and a small crew of contributors around him.'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'His readme carries something you rarely see, an AI disclosure. The foundation is handwritten, feature specs come from the developer, and any AI generated code is reviewed before merge.'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That kind of transparency about modern development is exactly what builds trust in 2026. The adoption number, straight from the project\'s own live counter, over 1.1 million container pulls on a project that\'s 7 months old.'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '1,608 stars, 71 forks, and the site proudly displays its own last commit timer. 15 hours ago when we recorded. The self-hosted music niche is small but fanatical and this has become its'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'discovery answer almost overnight. The feature set maps the whole discovery to playback loop. Discover feeds you recommendations and trends. Search previews tracks before you commit to space. Playlist is the killer wing.'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Scheduled flows that rotate like radio, a release radar that watches your artist, and importers for your Spotify, last.fm, or listenbrainz playlists. Bring your musical identity with you when you leave. Activity tracks the'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole download queue across Lidarr, YT-DLP, Soulseek, and Usenet. And it\'s properly multi-user, per user profiles and permissions, local auth, reverse proxy SSO, and native OIDC. Flows can'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'also convert into fixed track lists once you like a rotation. Radio that freezes into a mixtape on command. Setup is one compose block. The image from GitHub\'s registry, port 3001, and two volumes.'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Your config and the same media root Lidarr already mounts. That shared path detail is the one thing to get right, and the docs walk you through it. Bring the stack up, open the'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browser, create your admin account, connect Lidarr, done. There\'s a nightly channel if you like the bleeding edge, and a full example stack with Lidarr, Soulseek, and Navidrome in the repo. Total resource footprint is a single'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'node service with SQLite behind it. It will not be the heaviest thing on your server. The integration list reads like a self-hosted music census. Lidarr at the core, last.fm, listenbrainz, and Koyto feeding it your listening history.'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'YT-DLP, Soulseek, and SABnzbd or NZBGet moving the bits. Navidrome, Plex, and Plexamp playing them back through API synced playlists.'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ticketmaster for the nearby shows feature. Gotify and webhooks for notifications. It also stands on name shoulders. Musicbrainz for canonical metadata, and community projects Brainz Mash and Honker credited right in the'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Read Me. That credits table matters. Discovery projects usually scrape quietly and this one names and thanks its metadata sources instead. If you\'ve made the self-hosted music jump, this'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conversation is familiar. You export your library, set up Lyra and Navidrome, feel the ownership glow and 6 months later realize you haven\'t heard a new artist since. The streaming algorithm was doing real'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work and quitting it meant quitting discovery. Oral is the first project to treat that as the core problem instead of a nice-to-have. Day-to-day it\'s a phone experience. Browse the discover feed from the couch, preview a track, queue'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the album and it\'s in your library by the time you\'ve picked a playlist. Because playback runs through Guile, Navidrome or Plex, your existing apps including Plexamp just see new music appear. The marketing site is its own'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little Easter egg on mobile, too. Bull, a full retro music player widget with live project stats in the middle of it. There\'s LAN auto login, too, so the household never sees a password prompt'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on home Wi-Fi. The health check. 1,608 stars, 71 forks, 1.1 million pulls and just 26 open issues. On a project shipping this fast, that\'s a tracker being actively drained. The velocity'

